1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
An IPv4 address is thirty-two bits and an IPv6 address is a hundred and twenty-eight. The extra width is the reason the space stopped being countable.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
IPv4 has two to the thirty-two addresses, about 4.29 billion. IPv6 has two to the hundred and twenty-eight, about 3.4 times ten to the thirty-eight.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,000
By convention the hundred and twenty-eight bits split sixty-four and sixty-four: a routing prefix on the left and an interface ID on the right.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
A slash forty-eight site holds 65,536 subnets of size sixty-four, and a slash thirty-two allocation holds 4.29 billion of them.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
The address is written as eight groups of sixteen bits, separated by colons. Each group is four hexadecimal digits.

6
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Eight groups times sixteen bits is a hundred and twenty-eight bits, and four hex digits per group makes thirty-two hex digits in all.

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:51,000
One group, zero two zero two, is four zeros, then one, then four zeros, then one: sixteen bits in two nibbles of four.

8
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:58,000
The leading zeros are shown here only to line the boxes up. The written form drops them, and that is the first compression rule.

9
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Take the fully padded address, thirty-nine characters long, with three runs of zeros to deal with.

10
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Rule one drops the leading zeros inside every group. Rule two writes the hex digits a to f in lowercase.

11
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Rule three: the double colon may appear at most once, because two of them would make the address impossible to expand.

12
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Rule four compresses the longest run of zero groups. Here two runs tie at two groups each, so the rule needs a tie-break.

13
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Rule five: on a tie, compress the first run. So the canonical form is two thousand and one, db8, double colon, one, zero, zero, one.

14
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:39,000
To read an address back, count the groups on both sides of the double colon: two before, one after, so it stands for five groups of zeros.

15
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:46,000
The count must land on exactly eight groups. That single test accepts every legal address and rejects most malformed input.

16
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Two double colons, nine groups with none, five hex digits in a group: each is refused for the same reason, the count cannot be made to work.

17
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,000
A legal address can still be non-canonical. The padded form is valid and expands correctly; it is simply not how the standard writes it.

18
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,000
A prefix of length n leaves a hundred and twenty-eight minus n bits for the interface ID, so the subnet holds two to that power addresses.

19
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:16,000
A slash sixty-four is one subnet, a slash forty-eight site holds 65,536 of them, and a slash thirty-two allocation holds 4.29 billion.

20
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:24,000
One address in every subnet is spoken for: the all-zero interface ID is the subnet-router anycast address.

21
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:32,000
So a slash sixty-four has two to the sixty-four minus one usable interface IDs, and IPv6 reserves no broadcast address at all.

22
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:40,000
The base header is eight fields: version, traffic class, flow label, payload length, next header, hop limit, and the two addresses.

23
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Four, eight, twenty, sixteen, eight, eight, a hundred and twenty-eight, a hundred and twenty-eight. They sum to three hundred and twenty bits, which is forty octets.

24
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:57,000
Because the size is fixed there is no header-length field and no padding. The first two words hold the small fields and the rest is two addresses.

25
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Two field meanings to keep straight: hop limit counts hops and not seconds, and payload length counts octets after this header and not including it.

26
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:14,000
IPv4 carries a header checksum, and every router recomputes it because the time to live changed. IPv6 has no header checksum at all.

27
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:22,000
IPv4 options live inside the header, so its length varies. IPv6 moves them into extension headers and keeps the base header fixed.

28
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:29,000
IPv4 routers fragment. In IPv6 only the source fragments, and a router that cannot forward the packet sends ICMPv6 Packet Too Big, type two.

29
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:36,000
The minimum link MTU is 1280 octets, and time to live became hop limit because the standard no longer requires a packet lifetime.

30
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Build one packet: forty octets of base header, an eight-octet routing header, then a thousand-octet UDP datagram.

31
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Payload length is eight plus a thousand, so 1008. The base header is not counted and the extension header is.

32
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:58,000
On the wire the packet is 1048 octets. IPv4 would have written 1048 in total length, so the two fields answer different questions.

33
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Above 65,535 the field is set to zero and the jumbo payload option carries the length. TCP over IPv6 takes its MSS as the MTU minus sixty.

34
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:13,000
Next header names the header that follows this one, not the transport protocol directly.

35
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:20,000
The base header says zero, so hop-by-hop follows. That says forty-three, so routing follows. That says forty-four, so fragment. That says six, which is TCP.

36
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:27,000
The values worth knowing: zero, forty-three, forty-four, fifty and fifty-one, fifty-nine, sixty, six, seventeen and fifty-eight.

37
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:34,000
The order is prescribed: hop-by-hop first, then destination options, routing, fragment, and the security headers. A receiver processes them in that order.

38
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:42,000
A host can build its own interface ID from its hardware address. Start with the forty-eight-bit MAC address.

39
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Insert FF FE between the third and fourth octets, which turns six octets into eight.

40
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:57,000
Then flip the universal slash local bit, the zero two bit of the first octet. Zero zero becomes zero two, giving the interface ID.

41
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:04,000
Prefix it with the link-local prefix and the host has an address before anyone configures anything. Duplicate detection then runs.

42
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:12,000
GATE CSE 2018, question thirteen: match four fields to their lengths in bits. The IPv6 field in the list is next header.

43
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:20,000
Sixteen for a UDP port, forty-eight for a MAC address, eight for next header, thirty-two for a TCP sequence number. Option C.

44
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:28,000
The trap is the word next header. It is one octet because it carries a protocol number, the same field IPv4 calls protocol.

45
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:36,000
One caution: the question about IPv6 not supporting broadcast, tagged GATE CSE 2013 by several indexes, comes from ISRO CSE 2013.

